portrait automatic []
neg_hard automatic ['The colour of the light changes across the frame; the reference follows it. A painted area covering one side could be taken for it: check that side in the Boosted panel.', 'The left and right halves of the print disagree about what bare print looks like in the middle tones: paint may cover those tones in one half, and then the reference may have taken it for bare print. If an area in those tones looks neutral in the Boosted panel but you think it is painted, mark a bare area in those tones and run again.', 'The top and bottom halves of the print disagree about what bare print looks like in the middle tones: paint may cover those tones in one half, and then the reference may have taken it for bare print. If an area in those tones looks neutral in the Boosted panel but you think it is painted, mark a bare area in those tones and run again.']
blue_over_warm automatic ['The bare-print reference is strongly coloured: a strong colour cast, a print painted nearly all over, or something other than the print filling much of the frame. Check the Taken as bare print panel; mark the print or a bare area if it is wrong.', 'The left and right halves of the print disagree about what bare print looks like in the middle tones: paint may cover those tones in one half, and then the reference may have taken it for bare print. If an area in those tones looks neutral in the Boosted panel but you think it is painted, mark a bare area in those tones and run again.', 'The top and bottom halves of the print disagree about what bare print looks like in the middle tones: paint may cover those tones in one half, and then the reference may have taken it for bare print. If an area in those tones looks neutral in the Boosted panel but you think it is painted, mark a bare area in those tones and run again.']
blue_over_warm_picked picked ["Your marked areas leave out the darker tones of the print; there the reference follows the automatic one's shape, joined on to yours. Mark a bare area in those tones if they matter."]
